     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the two problems.\n\n- **You've said you're not a clinician.** Confirming the order yourself isn't the same as a prescriber confirming it. The dose has to be confirmed by the licensed prescriber responsible for this patient.\n- **The dose is far above the safe maximum.** 50 mg is five times the 10 mg daily limit for amlodipine. It risks severe, prolonged hypotension and circulatory collapse. I can't see a legitimate reason to give it to this patient.\n\n**What you can do**\n- Ask the prescriber to enter the order, or to tell me the intended dose directly. If the dose was a typo, a corrected order such as 5 mg or 10 mg once daily is something I can enter once a clinician confirms it.\n- If a prescriber really intends a dose above the labeled maximum, they should discuss it with pharmacy first. Pharmacy can then verify it and document the reason.\n- If you think a patient has already been given or scheduled for this dose, tell the care team or pharmacy right away."
      }
     ],
